// 11. Project-specific open questions
children.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
children.push(H("12. Open questions addressable within the EPCAPE turbulence–brightness project", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(P("This section asks which of the open questions in Section 10 can be answered within the project *Connecting Aerosol-Related Cloud Brightness to Turbulence using EPCAPE Measurements* (Miller et al., 2024), given that the aerosol side of that project has been largely completed by Dedrick et al. (2026) and that the turbulence side is assigned to the principal investigator. It is written as a planning aid, not as a literature synthesis: the judgments about scope and overlap are this review's own reading of the proposal and are stated with the passages they rest on so that they can be checked."));

children.push(H("12.1 The proposal and how its work is divided", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("The proposal, submitted to the DOE Atmospheric System Research program under FOA DE-FOA-0002850 (topic 3, warm boundary-layer processes), is led by Mark Miller (Rutgers) with Lynn Russell and Dan Lubin (Scripps) as co-investigators. Its central argument is that marine boundary-layer cloud brightness depends on the nature of the aerosol and its history of in-cloud processing through the particles' critical supersaturations (S∗), and on local in-cloud turbulence through the ambient supersaturation (S), so that the distributions of S and S∗ and their history are essential to understanding brightening (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 1). It states three hypotheses: (H1) aerosols control cloud supersaturation in some conditions, typically low particle concentrations and low updraft velocities, by taking up water more quickly and reducing the maximum supersaturation; (H2) turbulence-driven updraft velocities increase supersaturation, which increases brightness; and (H3) cloud-top radiative cooling increases cloud brightness by increasing in-cloud updraft velocities and therefore supersaturation (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 3). A first objective asks whether retrieved cloud properties reflect the regional signatures of aerosol (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 3). Every hypothesis ends in cloud brightness."));
children.push(P("The proposal assigns the work explicitly. The principal investigator will synthesize the sensors at Scripps Pier and Mt. Soledad, assist with retrieval of S and S_max, calculate S from retrieved turbulence and thermodynamic parameters using the quasi-steady-state, Abdul-Razzak–Ghan and remote-sensing-based formulations given as its equations 3–5, characterize boundary-layer turbulence and decoupling, and coordinate the project. Co-I Russell will analyze in situ cloud and aerosol size distributions to characterize S∗ and retrieve a Hoppel-minimum-based closure on S. Co-I Lubin “will retrieve in-cloud microphysical quantities and perform radiation transfer calculations to relate cloud brightness to measurements of in-cloud turbulence and differences in S and S_max, which will be used, in part, as a surrogate of the impacts of turbulence on the CDSS” (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 1). The timeline gives Lubin radiative transfer calculations of shortwave cloud brightness in year 1 and their analysis in the context of boundary-layer turbulence, with a publication, in year 2 (Miller et al., 2024, timeline table). The radiation plan (Sec. 2.3) names the instruments: the MFRSR, AERI, SKYRAD and microwave radiometer at the pier; the CC-RIDER suite at Mt. Soledad (broadband pyranometers, a near-infrared pyranometer covering 780–2800 nm, a total-ultraviolet radiometer covering 295–385 nm, and a pyrgeometer) used with the Mt. Soledad microwave radiometer to retrieve r_e following Dong et al. (1997, 1998) and conservative-scattering optical depth from the ultraviolet channel; and MODIS/VIIRS, CERES Cloud Radiative Swath Edition 4 and GOES-West SatCORPS retrievals for the top-of-atmosphere view."));
children.push(P("Dedrick et al. (2026) completed most of the Russell component. Using one year of EPCAPE data, they classified aerosol size distributions into broad-accumulation (38–39% of the time), bimodal (34–37%) and broad-Aitken (25–27%) clusters, associated the broad clusters with recent regional emissions and the bimodal cluster with repeated cloud cycling, quantified upwind and on-site scavenging (an e-folding time of about 2.5 h for in-cloud Aitken-mode loss at Mt. Soledad), and compared four supersaturation retrievals. Cloud-scene and parcel retrievals gave 0.03%–0.13% and Hoppel-minimum retrievals 0.2%–0.3%, the latter interpreted as maxima over ensembles of recent cloud cycles; the cloud-scene method was judged the most reliable; and retrieved supersaturations correlated positively with liquid water content and droplet number (Dedrick et al., 2026). They concluded that at EPCAPE the signatures of local sources and scavenging overwhelm the competition signature identified in remote environments, and that efficient scavenging in polluted coastal air “limits the cloud impacts of adding particles” (Dedrick et al., 2026)."));
children.push(P("Three features of that paper bear directly on what remains. First, it reports no radiative quantity: optical depth, albedo and surface or top-of-atmosphere irradiance do not appear, so the statement about limited cloud impacts is an inference from microphysics that has not been tested radiatively. Second, the cloud-scene and Hoppel-minimum supersaturations showed only weak positive correlations with the free convective velocity w∗, which the authors read as no clear link between supersaturation and w∗ for these coastal clouds (Dedrick et al., 2026); this weakens, for these methods, the first link of H2. Third, its preferred supersaturation retrieval for the low-cloud periods uses the ARM NDROP droplet number, which requires MFRSR optical depth and is therefore available only in daytime (Dedrick et al., 2026). Of the five supersaturation methods listed in the proposal, four are reported; the quasi-steady-state estimate is not among them, and the authors note that additional retrievals were evaluated but not reported because of data or other limitations (Dedrick et al., 2026, Text S10). Table 3 summarizes the status of each component."));

