children.push(H("12.2 Selection criteria", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("A question is listed below only if it meets five conditions: (1) it is open in the literature reviewed in Sections 9–10; (2) it falls within a task the proposal assigns to Co-I Lubin or leaves unassigned; (3) it uses the principal investigator's turbulence, updraft and coupling products only as inputs, for stratification or as covariates, rather than producing them; (4) it does not repeat Dedrick et al. (2026) or the published EPCAPE analysis of Zhang et al. (2025); and (5) it can be attempted with archived EPCAPE data. The candidates are ordered by how directly they answer the question the proposal leaves open after Dedrick et al. (2026), namely whether the aerosol and supersaturation differences that paper documents change cloud brightness, and then by feasibility."));

children.push(H("12.3 Candidate questions", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(PB("A. Does the brightness response to aerosol at EPCAPE show a diminished Twomey effect, and does it differ between aerosol regimes? ", "This asks for the albedo and optical-depth susceptibilities (dα_c/dln N_acc and dln τ/dln N_acc at fixed LWP) and the droplet-activation sensitivity (dln N_d/dln N_acc), stratified by the three size-distribution clusters of Dedrick et al. (2026) and by coupling state, followed where the data allow by the activation–competition decomposition that Dedrick et al. (2025) applied at LASIC. It addresses questions 5 and 11 of Section 10. At LASIC the competition response reduced the activation response to the Twomey effect by 12% using a parcel-based supersaturation and by up to 35% using the scene-based supersaturation, and it grew from clean to smoky conditions (Dedrick et al., 2025). Dedrick et al. (2026) inferred a different control at EPCAPE (scavenging rather than competition) but did not measure the radiative outcome. A theoretical expectation is available: the retrieved EPCAPE supersaturations of about 0.1% lie below the S_max of about 0.2% that Reutter et al. (2009) associate with the updraft-limited regime, in which N_d becomes insensitive to aerosol number; this placement is this review's inference, not a result of either paper. The testable prediction is therefore a smaller dln N_d/dln N_acc, and a smaller albedo susceptibility, in the polluted broad-mode clusters than in the bimodal cluster. A finding of no difference would also be informative, because it would contradict the scavenging-limits-impact inference. The question sits squarely in the proposal's first objective and H1, uses Lubin's retrievals and radiative transfer, and needs from the principal investigator's group only the coupling classification and, for the dynamical term of the decomposition, measured cloud-base updrafts, a term best computed jointly with that group. Data: the size-distribution and CCN data archived by the Russell group at the UC San Diego Library (see the data availability statement of Dedrick et al., 2026), MWR LWP, MFRSR optical depth and r_e, NDROP N_d, FM-120 droplet spectra at Mt. Soledad, SKYRAD and CC-RIDER irradiances, and libRadtran (Emde et al., 2016) to convert optical properties to albedo. The main risk is circularity: NDROP derives N_d from MFRSR optical depth and MWR LWP, and the cloud-scene supersaturation that Dedrick et al. (2026) prefer is built on NDROP, so regressing optical depth on that supersaturation is partly circular. Using aerosol number or CCN as the independent variable and the in situ FM-120 number as an independent check reduces this; Candidate B addresses it directly. A second risk is sample size: the proposal cites more than 700 hours with Mt. Soledad in cloud, but after the filters of Dedrick et al. (2026) there were 24 cloud events totalling 214 h and 374 h of low-cloud periods, before restriction to daytime, overcast, single-layer, coupled and non-drizzling scenes. Dedrick et al. (2025) also note that ACI_N carries uncertainty of up to 78% from the approximations used to derive N_d."));
children.push(PB("B. Which EPCAPE microphysical retrievals are independent and accurate enough for ACI analysis? ", "This asks for an error-characterized retrieval of optical depth, r_e and N_d from the CC-RIDER broadband and near-infrared pyranometers, the ultraviolet radiometer and the Mt. Soledad microwave radiometer, as the proposal plans (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.3; Dong et al., 1997, 1998), compared with the MFRSR, NDROP and lidar-based products and with the FM-120 droplet spectra measured in cloud at the same site. It addresses questions 7 and 18 of Section 10. The need is documented: an evaluation of four ground-based N_d retrievals against aircraft data at the Eastern North Atlantic site found that NDROP overestimates N_d for broken clouds or clouds with low LWP while agreeing for overcast clouds with LWP above about 25 g m⁻², that a radar-based retrieval was consistently low with a median error of about 35%, and that a micropulse-lidar method agreed best and works day and night (Zhang et al., 2023); NDROP was earlier found to produce distributions that are too broad at low LWP (Lim et al., 2016). EPCAPE operated a micropulse lidar (Russell et al., 2025), so a lidar-based N_d could also extend the cloud-scene supersaturation retrieval of Dedrick et al. (2026) to night. What EPCAPE adds that ENA lacked is a long record of in-cloud droplet spectra collocated with surface radiometers. The question is entirely within Lubin's task and does not touch the turbulence work. Its caveats are physical: when Mt. Soledad is in cloud its radiometers see only the part of the cloud above the site, and the FM-120 samples near the bottom of that column, whereas solar retrievals weight the upper cloud (Dong et al., 1998), so the comparison requires an explicit vertical profile model; and the proposal states that the ultraviolet radiometer data still required post-season calibration and algorithm validation at the time of writing (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.3), which should be confirmed with Co-I Lubin. The SCARPE campaign description also notes that without an independent LWP the optical depth is much less sensitive to r_e (ARM, EPCAPE-SCARPE campaign page), which is why the microwave radiometer at Mt. Soledad is essential."));
children.push(PB("C. How much does droplet-spectrum width offset the Twomey brightening at EPCAPE? ", "This asks how the relative dispersion of the droplet spectrum (σ_r/r̄) measured by the FM-120 varies with aerosol cluster, N_d and retrieved supersaturation, and how much the resulting change in spectral shape offsets or adds to the albedo change computed in Candidate A. Liu and Daum (2002) showed that aerosol-induced changes in spectral shape act to diminish the cooling of the Twomey effect, and the proposal notes that the albedo response can change with dispersion depending on the mechanism that produces it and that observed dispersion often exceeds what models produce (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 1, citing Feingold et al., 1997; Liu and Daum, 2002; Feingold and Siebert, 2009). The proposal lists the droplet number, mean radius and spectral width measured in cloud at Mt. Soledad among its variables (Miller et al., 2024, Table 2) and frames Lubin's supersaturation-based analysis as a surrogate for turbulence effects on the droplet spectrum. The question is therefore in scope, but it has the largest overlap risk of the five: the mechanism of turbulent broadening (supersaturation fluctuations, eddy hopping) belongs to the turbulence side of the project, so the defensible division is that this candidate quantifies the aerosol dependence and the radiative consequence of the measured width, using turbulence measures only as covariates. Caveats: the FM-120 covers 2–50 μm optical diameter (Dedrick et al., 2026), sampling is at one level near cloud base, and microphysical relationships in stratocumulus vary with height (Yeom et al., 2021), so a single-level dispersion may not represent the column that sets the albedo."));
children.push(PB("D. Is an aerosol signal detectable in surface shortwave transmission under coastal stratocumulus? ", "This is question 8 of Section 10, restated for the project. The only long-term surface study found no daily aerosol signal at the Southern Great Plains, where LWP explained most of the variance (Sena et al., 2016). The design that makes either outcome publishable is to compute the expected transmission change per unit change in ln N_acc at fixed LWP and solar zenith angle with libRadtran before examining the data, then test overcast, single-layer, coupled, non-drizzling scenes from SKYRAD at the pier and CC-RIDER at Mt. Soledad against that expectation. The proposal's own argument that the near-infrared pyranometer is more sensitive to r_e than a broadband instrument, because liquid water absorbs in that band (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.3), offers a second aerosol-sensitive quantity, the near-infrared-to-broadband ratio at fixed LWP, that does not depend on any retrieval product. The candidate is within Lubin's radiometric task and does not involve the turbulence work. It can stand alone or be folded into Candidate A as a retrieval-independent radiative metric. The risk is that the signal is below the noise set by LWP variability; Section 8 notes that the transmission-susceptibility expression used there to estimate its size is this review's own extrapolation and must be replaced by explicit radiative transfer."));
children.push(PB("E. Is the EPCAPE brightness response visible at the top of the atmosphere, and does it depend on mesoscale morphology? ", "This asks for top-of-atmosphere albedo computed from the surface-retrieved cloud properties and compared with CERES and GOES SatCORPS retrievals for matched scenes, and for the susceptibilities from Candidate A recomputed from the satellite view and stratified by cloud morphology. It addresses questions 1, 11 and 17 of Section 10. ACI metrics depend on averaging scale (McComiskey and Feingold, 2012), and susceptibilities vary within stratocumulus regimes with cloud morphology (Goren et al., 2026), so whether a point-based surface susceptibility reproduces the satellite one is an open and testable question. It is in scope: the proposal states that “if turbulent effects are robust, we should expect to detect them in CERES TOA observations” and plans to examine mesoscale organization and its dependence on aerosol loading (Miller et al., 2024, Secs. 2.3 and 3). It also produces a time-resolved brightness series that the principal investigator's group can use to test H2, which is a contribution to, not a duplication of, that work. Caveats: the 20-km CERES footprint mixes land and ocean at this coastal site, GOES retrievals are half-hourly, and the proposal shows that orography modifies turbulence at Mt. Soledad on some days (Miller et al., 2024, Fig. 1)."));

children.push(H("12.4 The boundary with the turbulence work", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("H2 and H3 both end in brightness, and brightness comes only from the radiation side of the project, so the boundary is best drawn by product rather than by hypothesis. On the principal investigator's side are turbulence profiles from Doppler lidar and radar, decoupling diagnostics, cloud-base updraft distributions and supersaturation computed from them with the proposal's equations 3–5; the PI has previously published on the joint turbulence and radiation structure of stratocumulus-topped boundary layers (Ghate et al., 2014), which supports treating the turbulence half of H3 as his. On the radiation side are calibrated optical depth, albedo and irradiance time series at the surface, at cloud level and at the top of the atmosphere, and the cloud-top shortwave heating and longwave cooling computed from retrieved cloud properties and soundings, which the proposal lists as a computed variable (Miller et al., 2024, Table 2) and which H3 requires as input. Two observations suggest that the joint test of H2 should allow for a path through LWP rather than only through supersaturation: Dedrick et al. (2026) found no clear link between supersaturation and w∗ for these clouds, and the proposal's preliminary ultraviolet-radiometer result suggested that stronger sub-cloud turbulence was associated with larger optical depth after sunrise and smaller optical depth in the early afternoon, with the caution that those data were not yet calibrated (Miller et al., 2024, Fig. 5)."));
children.push(P("Four lines of work are not recommended because they duplicate completed or assigned work or fall outside the proposal: re-deriving supersaturation from Hoppel minima or CCN closure (Dedrick et al., 2026); deriving turbulence statistics, decoupling diagnostics or turbulence-based supersaturation (assigned to the principal investigator); repeating the machine-learning LWP-adjustment analysis of Zhang et al. (2025) without a new angle; and drizzle or precipitation processes, which the proposal's case-selection criteria exclude (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.1)."));

children.push(H("12.5 Summary and recommended sequence", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
